{"content":[{"c":["ZERED"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Valley and brook by which the Israelites encamped, listed between Iye-abarim and a stopping place near the Arnon River to the north (",{"href":"?bref=Num.21.12","r":"Num.21.12","rawR":"Num.21.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.21.12","value":"Num.21.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 21:12"},"). Though its exact location remains in question, Zered is probably identifiable with the modern Wadi el-Hesa, a streambed that formed a natural border between the ancient countries of Moab and Edom and, following a northwestward course, emptied into the southern end of the Dead Sea. The crossing of the Zered Brook by the Israelites marked 38 years since Israel rebelled against God at Kadesh-barnea (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.2.13-14","r":"Deut.2.13-14","rawR":"Deut.2.13-14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.2.13-14","value":"Deut.2.13-14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dt 2:13-14"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Zered","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Nm 21:12","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Num.21.12","rawQueryValue":"Num.21.12","sourceRefExact":"Num.21.12"},{"anchorText":"Dt 2:13-14","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Deut.2.13-14","rawQueryValue":"Deut.2.13-14","sourceRefExact":"Deut.2.13-14"}],"sourceItemFragmentSha256":"44deeaba202768093f4fe8978709be417ba428b9e4ff899af03463e2845009ea","sourceItemName":"Zered","sourceItemOrder":85,"sourceMember":"Articles/Z.xml","supplementPointersNotIncludedAsContent":[]},"sourceAttributes":{"name":"Zered","product":"TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","typename":"Article"},"sourceTitleExact":"Zered","title":"Zered","version":"v2-a101d843a2f0cd3fc0c6"}